    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 14 with a potent core, anchored by **Yordan Alvarez** (70.64) and **Christian Yelich** (69.85), our top two plays for the day. **Yordan Alvarez** benefits from a favorable matchup against Luis Castillo, leveraging an elite .421 xwOBA to maintain a high floor despite a slight park penalty. Meanwhile, **Christian Yelich** draws a prime opportunity against Griffin Canning; his score is significantly bolstered by a 13% dynamic platoon advantage, making him a high-leverage utility play. **Kyle Schwarber** (67.60) also merits attention, as he continues to thrive in favorable park conditions, pairing his elite .401 xwOBA with a productive leadoff spot. Despite facing the lefty Ranger Suarez, the projected run-scoring environment keeps **Kyle Schwarber** and **Trea Turner** (65.75) locked into our top-tier starting slots.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Caleb Durbin** (40.37), who narrowly missed the cut. While **Caleb Durbin** possesses the speed profile we covet, the optimizer flagged a sharp drop-off due to his projected #9 spot in the order and a tough matchup against Jes\u00fas Luzardo, whose 3.21 xERA suppresses the viability of fringe starters. We are opting for depth and platoon-heavy matchups elsewhere, keeping **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (41.99) in the lineup despite the heavy penalty against Chris Sale, simply because his athleticism remains a necessary variable for our stolen base targets. Owners should exercise caution with **Colt Keith** (44.21) and **Casey Schmitt** (44.87); both face formidable arms in Nolan McLean and Emmet Sheehan, respectively, and are trending toward the bottom of our efficiency threshold. With all lineups still officially pending, keep a close eye on the confirmed batting orders, as any move down the lineup for our marginal starters could necessitate immediate re-evaluation to preserve our season-long game cap efficiency.",
